<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-applications-integration">
    <title>Applications of Integration</title>

    <section xml:id="sec-area-between-curves">
        <title>Areas Between Curves</title>

        <p>
            Definite integrals can be used to compute the area of a region
            bounded by two curves. Suppose that <m>f(x)</m> lies above
            <m>g(x)</m> on the interval <m>[a,b]</m>. We can divide the
            interval into narrow vertical strips. The approximate area of
            one strip is
        </p>

        <me>
            \left(f(x_i)-g(x_i)\right)\Delta x.
        </me>

        <p>
            Summing these strips and taking the limit gives
        </p>

        <me>
            A=\int_a^b \left(f(x)-g(x)\right)\,dx.
        </me>

        <remark xml:id="rem-top-minus-bottom">
            <title>Top function minus bottom function</title>

            <p>
                When using vertical slices, the area between two curves is
                found by integrating the top function minus the bottom
                function.
            </p>
        </remark>

        <example xml:id="ex-area-exp-parabola">
            <title>Area between an exponential and a parabola</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the area between
                </p>

                <me>
                    y=e^x
                </me>

                <p>
                    and
                </p>

                <me>
                    y=x^2-2
                </me>

                <p>
                    from <m>x=-1</m> to <m>x=2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    On the interval shown, the upper function is
                    <m>e^x</m> and the lower function is
                    <m>x^2-2</m>. Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    A
                    =
                    \int_{-1}^{2}
                    \left(e^x-(x^2-2)\right)\,dx.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Simplifying the integrand,
                </p>

                <me>
                    A
                    =
                    \int_{-1}^{2}
                    \left(e^x-x^2+2\right)\,dx.
                </me>

                <p>
                    An antiderivative is
                </p>

                <me>
                    e^x-\frac{1}{3}x^3+2x.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Hence
                </p>

                <me>
                    A
                    =
                    \left[
                    e^x-\frac{1}{3}x^3+2x
                    \right]_{-1}^{2}.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Evaluating,
                </p>

                <me>
                    A
                    =
                    e^2-\frac{8}{3}+4
                    -
                    \left(
                    e^{-1}+\frac{1}{3}-2
                    \right),
                </me>

                <p>
                    so
                </p>

                <me>
                    A=e^2+3-e^{-1}.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-area-sqrt-line">
            <title>Finding a bounded area</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the area in the first quadrant bounded by
                </p>

                <me>
                    y=2
                    \qquad\text{and}\qquad
                    y=\sqrt{x}.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    First find the point of intersection:
                </p>

                <me>
                    2=\sqrt{x}.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Squaring both sides gives
                </p>

                <me>
                    x=4.
                </me>

                <p>
                    The upper function is <m>2</m> and the lower function is
                    <m>\sqrt{x}</m>. Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    A
                    =
                    \int_0^4
                    \left(2-\sqrt{x}\right)\,dx.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Evaluating,
                </p>

                <me>
                    A
                    =
                    \left[
                    2x-\frac{2}{3}x^{3/2}
                    \right]_0^4
                    =
                    8-\frac{16}{3}
                    =
                    \frac{8}{3}.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-cumulative-change">
        <title>Cumulative Change</title>

        <p>
            When a function represents a rate of change, its definite integral
            gives the cumulative change in the underlying quantity.
        </p>

        <p>
            If <m>f(t)</m> is a rate of change, then the cumulative change
            from time <m>t=a</m> to time <m>t=b</m> is
        </p>

        <me>
            \int_a^b f(t)\,dt.
        </me>

        <p>
            The rate <m>f(t)</m> can be negative. In that case, the negative
            contribution to the integral corresponds to a decrease in the
            underlying quantity.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="ex-cumulative-population">
            <title>Population growth</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider a population whose size at time <m>t</m> is
                    <m>N(t)</m> and whose growth obeys
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{dN}{dt}=e^{-t},
                </me>

                <p>
                    with
                </p>

                <me>
                    N(0)=100.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Find the cumulative change in population from
                    <m>t=0</m> to <m>t=5</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The cumulative change is
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_0^5 e^{-t}\,dt.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_0^5 e^{-t}\,dt
                    =
                    \left[-e^{-t}\right]_0^5
                    =
                    1-e^{-5}.
                </me>

                <p>
                    We can verify this result by solving the initial value
                    problem. Antidifferentiating gives
                </p>

                <me>
                    N(t)=-e^{-t}+C.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Using <m>N(0)=100</m>,
                </p>

                <me>
                    100=-1+C,
                </me>

                <p>
                    so <m>C=101</m>. Hence
                </p>

                <me>
                    N(t)=101-e^{-t}.
                </me>

                <p>
                    The change from <m>t=0</m> to <m>t=5</m> is therefore
                </p>

                <me>
                    N(5)-N(0)=1-e^{-5},
                </me>

                <p>
                    which agrees with the definite integral.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-cumulative-rainfall">
            <title>Accumulated rainfall</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    A rain gauge measures the rate of rainfall in millimeters
                    per hour. Suppose the rainfall rate is
                </p>

                <me>
                    r(t)=3\left(1-\cos(2\pi t)\right).
                </me>

                <p>
                    Find the total rainfall between time <m>0</m> and time
                    <m>T</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Total rainfall is the integral of the rainfall rate:
                </p>

                <me>
                    R(T)
                    =
                    \int_0^T
                    3\left(1-\cos(2\pi t)\right)\,dt.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    R(T)
                    =
                    3
                    \left[
                    t-\frac{1}{2\pi}\sin(2\pi t)
                    \right]_0^T.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Hence
                </p>

                <me>
                    R(T)
                    =
                    3\left(
                    T-\frac{1}{2\pi}\sin(2\pi T)
                    \right).
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-average-values">
        <title>Average Values</title>

        <p>
            For a finite collection of values
            <m>f(x_1),\ldots,f(x_n)</m>, the ordinary arithmetic average is
        </p>

        <me>
            \frac{1}{n}
            \sum_{i=1}^n f(x_i).
        </me>

        <p>
            We can extend this idea to a continuous function on an interval.
        </p>

        <definition xml:id="def-average-value">
            <title>Average Value of a Function</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    The average value of a continuous function <m>f</m> on
                    <m>[a,b]</m> is
                </p>

                <me>
                    f_{\mathrm{avg}}
                    =
                    \frac{1}{b-a}
                    \int_a^b f(x)\,dx.
                </me>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <p>
            Geometrically,
        </p>

        <me>
            f_{\mathrm{avg}}(b-a)
            =
            \int_a^b f(x)\,dx.
        </me>

        <p>
            Thus, <m>f_{\mathrm{avg}}</m> is the height of a rectangle with
            width <m>b-a</m> having the same signed area as the region
            represented by the integral.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="ex-average-x2">
            <title>Average value of <m>x^2</m></title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the average value of
                    <m>f(x)=x^2</m> on the interval <m>[0,2]</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    By the definition of average value,
                </p>

                <me>
                    f_{\mathrm{avg}}
                    =
                    \frac{1}{2-0}
                    \int_0^2 x^2\,dx.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    f_{\mathrm{avg}}
                    =
                    \frac{1}{2}
                    \left[
                    \frac{1}{3}x^3
                    \right]_0^2
                    =
                    \frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{8}{3}
                    =
                    \frac{4}{3}.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <p>
            Examples of continuous averages include average speed over a time
            interval, average height over a region of terrain, and average
            temperature over the course of a day.
        </p>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-mean-value-theorem-integrals">
        <title>The Mean Value Theorem for Integrals</title>

        <p>
            If a continuous function has an average value on an interval,
            it is natural to ask whether the function actually takes that
            value somewhere on the interval.
        </p>

        <theorem xml:id="thm-mean-value-integrals">
            <title>Mean Value Theorem for Integrals</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>f</m> is continuous on <m>[a,b]</m>, then there
                    exists some <m>c</m> in <m>[a,b]</m> such that
                </p>

                <me>
                    f(c)(b-a)
                    =
                    \int_a^b f(x)\,dx.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Equivalently,
                </p>

                <me>
                    f(c)=f_{\mathrm{avg}}.
                </me>
            </statement>
        </theorem>

        <p>
            In words, a continuous function takes on its average value at
            least once somewhere in the interval.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="ex-mvt-x3">
            <title>Finding where a function equals its average value</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine the average value of
                    <m>f(x)=x^3</m> on <m>[0,1]</m>, and find a value of
                    <m>x</m> where <m>f(x)</m> equals this average.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The average value is
                </p>

                <me>
                    f_{\mathrm{avg}}
                    =
                    \int_0^1 x^3\,dx
                    =
                    \left[
                    \frac{1}{4}x^4
                    \right]_0^1
                    =
                    \frac{1}{4}.
                </me>

                <p>
                    We therefore solve
                </p>

                <me>
                    x^3=\frac{1}{4}.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Hence
                </p>

                <me>
                    x=\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^{1/3}.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-volumes">
        <title>Volumes of Solids</title>

        <p>
            Definite integrals can also be used to calculate volumes.
            Suppose the region under <m>y=f(x)</m> from <m>x=a</m> to
            <m>x=b</m> is rotated around the horizontal axis.
        </p>

        <p>
            A thin vertical slice generates a disk with radius
            approximately <m>f(x_i)</m> and thickness <m>\Delta x</m>.
            Its volume is approximately
        </p>

        <me>
            \pi\left(f(x_i)\right)^2\Delta x.
        </me>

        <p>
            Adding the disk volumes and taking a limit gives the disk method.
        </p>

        <theorem xml:id="thm-disk-method">
            <title>Disk Method</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    If the region under <m>y=f(x)</m> on <m>[a,b]</m>
                    is rotated about the horizontal axis, its volume is
                </p>

                <me>
                    V
                    =
                    \pi
                    \int_a^b
                    \left(f(x)\right)^2\,dx.
                </me>
            </statement>
        </theorem>

        <example xml:id="ex-volume-sqrt">
            <title>A volume using the disk method</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the volume of the solid obtained by rotating the
                    region bounded by
                </p>

                <me>
                    y=\sqrt{x-1},
                    \qquad
                    x=2,
                    \qquad
                    x=5,
                    \qquad
                    y=0
                </me>

                <p>
                    about the horizontal axis.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The radius of each disk is
                    <m>\sqrt{x-1}</m>. Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    V
                    =
                    \pi
                    \int_2^5
                    \left(\sqrt{x-1}\right)^2\,dx.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Simplifying,
                </p>

                <me>
                    V
                    =
                    \pi
                    \int_2^5
                    (x-1)\,dx.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Thus,
                </p>

                <me>
                    V
                    =
                    \pi
                    \left[
                    \frac{1}{2}x^2-x
                    \right]_2^5
                    =
                    \frac{15\pi}{2}.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-volume-sphere">
            <title>Recovering the volume of a sphere</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    The upper semicircle of radius <m>r</m> is
                </p>

                <me>
                    y=\sqrt{r^2-x^2}.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Rotate this semicircle around the horizontal axis to
                    calculate the volume of a sphere of radius <m>r</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Using the disk method,
                </p>

                <me>
                    V
                    =
                    \pi
                    \int_{-r}^{r}
                    \left(\sqrt{r^2-x^2}\right)^2\,dx.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    V
                    =
                    \pi
                    \int_{-r}^{r}
                    (r^2-x^2)\,dx.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Evaluating,
                </p>

                <me>
                    V
                    =
                    \pi
                    \left[
                    r^2x-\frac{1}{3}x^3
                    \right]_{-r}^{r}
                    =
                    \frac{4}{3}\pi r^3.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <p>
            If the region being rotated does not touch the axis of rotation,
            each cross-section has a hole. The resulting cross-sections are
            washers rather than disks.
        </p>

        <theorem xml:id="thm-washer-method">
            <title>Washer Method</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>R(x)</m> is the outer radius and <m>r(x)</m> is
                    the inner radius, then the volume is
                </p>

                <me>
                    V
                    =
                    \pi
                    \int_a^b
                    \left(
                    R(x)^2-r(x)^2
                    \right)\,dx.
                </me>
            </statement>
        </theorem>

        <example xml:id="ex-washer-sqrt-line">
            <title>A volume using washers</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Rotate the region bounded by
                </p>

                <me>
                    y=\sqrt{x}
                    \qquad\text{and}\qquad
                    y=\frac{x}{2}
                </me>

                <p>
                    about the horizontal axis and compute the volume.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    First find the intersection points:
                </p>

                <me>
                    \sqrt{x}=\frac{x}{2}.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Squaring gives
                </p>

                <me>
                    x=\frac{x^2}{4},
                </me>

                <p>
                    so
                </p>

                <me>
                    x=0
                    \qquad\text{or}\qquad
                    x=4.
                </me>

                <p>
                    On <m>[0,4]</m>, the outer radius is
                    <m>\sqrt{x}</m> and the inner radius is
                    <m>x/2</m>. Thus,
                </p>

                <me>
                    V
                    =
                    \pi
                    \int_0^4
                    \left[
                    (\sqrt{x})^2
                    -
                    \left(\frac{x}{2}\right)^2
                    \right]\,dx.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    V
                    =
                    \pi
                    \int_0^4
                    \left(
                    x-\frac{x^2}{4}
                    \right)\,dx.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Evaluating,
                </p>

                <me>
                    V
                    =
                    \pi
                    \left[
                    \frac{x^2}{2}
                    -
                    \frac{x^3}{12}
                    \right]_0^4
                    =
                    \frac{8\pi}{3}.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-arc-length">
        <title>Arc Length</title>

        <p>
            Integration can also be used to determine the length of a curved
            graph. Suppose we want to find the length of
            <m>y=f(x)</m> from <m>x=a</m> to <m>x=b</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Divide the interval <m>[a,b]</m> into many small subintervals.
            Over a sufficiently small subinterval, the curve is approximately
            a straight line.
        </p>

        <p>
            If the horizontal change is <m>\Delta x</m>, then the
            corresponding vertical change is approximately
        </p>

        <me>
            \Delta y
            \approx
            f'(x_i)\Delta x.
        </me>

        <p>
            By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the small line segment
            is approximately
        </p>

        <me>
            \sqrt{
            (\Delta x)^2
            +
            (\Delta y)^2
            }.
        </me>

        <p>
            Substituting
            <m>\Delta y\approx f'(x_i)\Delta x</m> gives
        </p>

        <me>
            \Delta x
            \sqrt{
            1+\left(f'(x_i)\right)^2
            }.
        </me>

        <p>
            Summing these small lengths gives
        </p>

        <me>
            L
            \approx
            \sum_i
            \Delta x
            \sqrt{
            1+\left(f'(x_i)\right)^2
            }.
        </me>

        <p>
            Taking the limit produces the arc-length formula.
        </p>

        <theorem xml:id="thm-arc-length">
            <title>Arc Length Formula</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>f'</m> is continuous on <m>[a,b]</m>, then the
                    length of the graph <m>y=f(x)</m> from <m>x=a</m> to
                    <m>x=b</m> is
                </p>

                <me>
                    L
                    =
                    \int_a^b
                    \sqrt{
                    1+\left(f'(x)\right)^2
                    }
                    \,dx.
                </me>
            </statement>
        </theorem>

        <example xml:id="ex-arc-length-power">
            <title>Finding the length of a curve</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the length of the curve
                </p>

                <me>
                    y=\frac{2}{3}x^{3/2}
                </me>

                <p>
                    from <m>x=0</m> to <m>x=4</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    First differentiate:
                </p>

                <me>
                    f'(x)=x^{1/2}.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    L
                    =
                    \int_0^4
                    \sqrt{1+x}\,dx.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Evaluating,
                </p>

                <me>
                    L
                    =
                    \left[
                    \frac{2}{3}(1+x)^{3/2}
                    \right]_0^4.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Hence
                </p>

                <me>
                    L
                    =
                    \frac{2}{3}
                    \left(
                    5^{3/2}-1
                    \right).
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

    </section>

</chapter>